BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tea and Entertainment
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260918T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260918T160000
UID:6a77377a4523092f0035a90e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Enjoy watching DVDs in the Library Auditorium.\nCoffee\, tea a
 nd cookies or snacks will be served after the film.  Ingredient lists will
  be available upon request.   .\nMystery of Eels\nThe eel—snakelike\, se
 cretive\, and lined with sharp teeth—remains one of the natural world’
 s great mysteries\, with scientists still unsure where it travels\, how it
  lives\, and even how it dies. Long celebrated in poetry and legend as a s
 trange and alluring creature\, the eel continues to fascinate researchers 
 who are trying to uncover the secrets of its hidden journey. Closed captio
 ns. 60 minutes.2013.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a77377a4523092f0035a90e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Digital Privacy 201
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261004T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261004T150000
UID:6a721a251d5d03c3a6baf4cb@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This is the second session in the Digital Privacy 101 series a
 nd is intended for participants who have completed Part 1. Learn practical
  strategies to keep your personal information safe online\, including esse
 ntial digital privacy habits\, creating and managing strong passwords\, an
 d recognizing common online threats such as phishing\, malware\, and hacki
 ng. Gain the knowledge and confidence to make safer choices online.\nRegis
 ter online. Drop-ins welcome if space is available.
LOCATION:Steeles
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a721a251d5d03c3a6baf4cb
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Seniors Club - Birds in Winter
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261117T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261117T150000
UID:6a6cb34888e9bf28003497f0@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Cliffcrest invites all to our Seniors Club! Hang out and enjoy
  games\, engaging presentations and workshops on a variety of topics.\nThi
 s Month: Birds in Winter\nWinter is a harsh season\, yet many birds stay i
 n Toronto and survive. Why don’t they all fly south? How do they stay wa
 rm and find food and shelter? Which birds stay and which ones migrate? Lea
 rn about migrant birds and their incredible journeys\, visiting birds who 
 come to the city from their boreal and Arctic homes\, and our resident bir
 ds who spend the long\, cold winter months here.\nJoin Monika Croydon\, av
 id local birder and member of the Toronto Ornithological Club\, to find ou
 t how we can all help birds in winter\, from providing feeders and creatin
 g bird-friendly gardens and homes\, to supporting conservation and researc
 h as Citizen Scientists.
LOCATION:Cliffcrest
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6cb34888e9bf28003497f0
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Resilience Reads: a climate neighbourhood reading party
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260916T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260916T200000
UID:69dd3dd5c1cf6f9a758518d0@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us to share the joy of reading\, while building climate r
 esilience knowledge. Members are invited to discuss a book they are readin
 g\, tell stories\,  and empower each other to explore community-driven sol
 utions. Each party will start with a quiet read and reflect time\, with op
 portunities to join group discussions later on over refreshments.\nAPRIL 1
 5-Learning from trees for Earth Month\nThe Overstory\nby Richard Power\nMA
 Y 20-All women are water defenders\nMemory of Water\nby Emmi Itaranta or y
 our favourite book featuring water!\nJUNE 17– Righting our relationship 
 with land\nEarth Keeper\nby N. Scott Momaday or your favourite book that s
 hares how words are medicine!\nJULY 15– Climate migration or the borders
  between us are not lines that divide us\, but seams that connect us\nGun 
 Island\n/\nThe Hungry Tide\nby Amitav Ghosh or your favourite island setti
 ng summer read!\nCopies are available in the branch to pick up\, while sup
 plies last.\nAUGUST 19--Fossil Fuel Folly\nAmerican War\nby Omar El Akkad
  or your favourite dystopian read!\nSEPTEMBER 16--We Need Pollinators\nTh
 e History of Bees\nby Maja Lunde or your favourite animal-centred book!\n
 OCTOBER 21--Climate Change is Scary\nThe Annual Migration of Clouds\nby Pr
 emee Mohamed or a different book that shares a scary future ecological cri
 sis!\nNOVEMBER 18--Climate changes everything\nThe Ministry for the Future
 \nby Kim Stanley Robinson or your favourite time travel read!\nInformatio
 n Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected b
 y the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(
 a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and pr
 ogram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the libr
 ary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswe
 rline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Parkdale\, Auditorium A
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69dd3dd5c1cf6f9a758518d0
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
