BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:How to Avoid Legal Problems
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T150000
UID:6a8f2591b20478002995b2d9@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Learn how to be better prepared to manage future legal issues.
  Retired lawyer\, Sheldon Parker\, offers general information for older ad
 ults on three key topics that may likely affect them in the future: Powers
  of Attorney\, Will and Codicils\, and Real Estate Ownership Issues.\nA gr
 aduate of Osgoode Hall Law School\, Sheldon Parker was a founding partner 
 of Parker & Zener LLP\, a law firm specializing in Real Estate\, Commercia
 l\, Tax and Estate work. Retired from active practice in early 2016\, Shel
 don now lectures on politics\, fraud\, elder abuse and Estate planning thr
 oughout the GTA.
LOCATION:Yorkville
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8f2591b20478002995b2d9
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Investing Basics
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260915T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260915T193000
UID:69ecde80b1165a41a66198a2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:New to investing? Learn the basics of this important part of f
 inancial literacy\, including how to save with the goal of investing\, typ
 es of investments\, how to open an account\, types of accounts\, how to av
 oid financial fraud and scams\, and more.\nPresented by Canadian Investmen
 t Regulatory Organization (CIRO)\, a national self-regulatory organization
  that oversees all investment dealers\, mutual fund dealers and trading ac
 tivity on Canada’s debt and equity marketplaces\nPlease register with yo
 ur phone number if you want to be reminded with a phone call. One registra
 tion per person. Do not register twice\, contact staff to confirm if neede
 d.\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Publ
 ic Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 2
 0(d). The information will only be used for event and program registration
 \, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your i
 nformation can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-
 397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Bridlewood\, Meeting Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ecde80b1165a41a66198a2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Mount Pleasant Cemetery History (evening presentation)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261015T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261015T193000
UID:6a3c23260e851028002cec18@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The Sunnyside Historic Society Presents stories from the crypt
 !\nWhat is the connection between a survivor of the Titanic\, a world clas
 s pianist\, and a prime minister? T\nhey were all buried at Mount Pleasant
  Cemetery.\nJoin us for a combination of story-telling\, pictures\, and sh
 ort skits - on the history of Mount Pleasant Cemetery and the souls burie
 d there.\nNote: This program will run twice on the same day\, from 2-3:30p
 m and 6-7:30pm\, both being the same presentation. Please sign up for a ti
 me that suits you best.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal info
 rmation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the 
 Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information
  will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evalu
 ation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be dir
 ected to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yon
 ge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Mount Pleasant\, Basement program room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3c23260e851028002cec18
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Digital Expo: AI & Democracy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261003T093000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261003T163000
UID:6a904cc4be148200298a6f8c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join leading experts for thought-provoking panel discussions e
 xploring the impact of AI on safety\, democracy\, civic participation\, tr
 ust and human flourishing. This registration provides access to all four 
 panel discussions and the exhibitor showcase.\nCan't attend in person? The
  panels will also be livestreamed.\nRegister to receive the viewing link\n
 .\nPanel discussions\nHelping Children Navigate AI Safely\n11:15 am-12 p
 m\nExplore how AI is shaping children's lives and learn practical ways to 
 support safe\, responsible and informed AI use at home and in school.\nFea
 turing Matthew Johnson (MediaSmarts)\, André Côté (The Dais)\, and Aara
 ni Stoneham (AI Literacy Learners)\, with moderation by Jessica Wong (CBC)
 .\nSafeguarding Democracy in the Age of AI\n1-1:45 pm\nJoin this keynote t
 o examine the opportunities and risks AI presents for democracy\, and the 
 steps needed to strengthen trust\, safety and civic participation.\nFeatur
 ing Beatrice Wayne (Samara Centre for Democracy) and Valentine Goddard (AI
  Impact Alliance).\nAI and Civic Engagement\n2-3 pm\nLearn how AI is chang
 ing the way people access information\, engage with one another and partic
 ipate in democracy.\nFeaturing Mathew Markman (Ethelo)\, Trevor Potts (Evi
 dence for Democracy) and Lexa Michaelides (Hypha)\, with moderation by Tai
  Huynh (The Local).\nFlourishing in an AI Society\n3:15-4:15 pm\nDiscove
 r how communities can help guide AI development toward human flourishing\,
  trust and democratic values.\nFeaturing Victória Oldemburgo de Mello (Wo
 rk and Play Lab)\, Jess Mitchell (OCAD University)\, Stephanie Jonnson (OD
 LAN) and Mario Vasilescu (Readocracy)\, with moderation by Pete Bombaci (G
 enwell).\nExhibitor showcase\n9:30 am-4:30 pm\nConnect with educators and 
 agencies\, and discover the latest tools\, resources\, and solutions desig
 ned to empower individuals with essential digital skills.\nAI Literacy Lea
 rners\nArts Etobicoke\nCompetition Bureau Canada\nConnectTO\nDigital Momen
 t\nGenwell: Human Connection Movement\nMediaSmarts\nOpen Digital Literacy 
 and Access Network (ODLAN)\nPop-Up Learning Labs\nReadocracy\nTELUS Wise\n
 ***\nAbout this event\nThis year's Digital Expo is a dynamic day of learn
 ing\, exploration and connection focused on shaping a responsible AI futur
 e for everyone.\nFeaturing inspiring speakers and interactive exhibits\, a
 s well as hands-on workshops for adults\, kids\, and teens\, the event exp
 lores how emerging technologies are transforming our lives\, communities a
 nd civic institutions.\nTogether\, we'll examine the opportunities and cha
 llenges of AI to foster a more informed\, inclusive and empowered digital 
 society that protects Canadians and safeguards democracy.\nSeparate regist
 ration is required for each workshop. Check out\ntpl.ca/digitalexpo\nfor m
 ore information about the Expo and the workshops.\n***\nInformation Collec
 tion Notice\n: The personal information on this form is collected by the 
 Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 2
 0(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program 
 registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library ha
 ndles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@t
 pl.ca\, 416-3975981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.\n***
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a904cc4be148200298a6f8c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tuesday Night Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T191500
UID:6a9050fd57c264087ad42f94@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Please join us on Tuesday\, September 29 at 6:30 p.m. to talk 
 about the novel\nMedicine walk\nby Richard Wagamese.\nSet in the dramatic 
 landscape of the BC Interior\, 16-year-old Franklin Starlight is called to
  visit his father\, Eldon\, a man he barely knows. He finds Eldon dying of
  liver failure after years of heavy drinking. Eldon asks his son to take h
 im into the mountains\, so he may be buried in the traditional Ojibway man
 ner. This is a novel about love\, friendship\, courage\, and the idea that
  the land has within it powers of healing.\nYou can get a copy at the firs
 t-floor circulation desk while supplies last. If you prefer an eBook or eA
 udiobook\, ask library staff how to access them.\nDrop-in\, registration n
 ot required. For more information\, call: 416-394-1000.
LOCATION:Maria A. Shchuka
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9050fd57c264087ad42f94
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:ACT-ively Preventing Elder Abuse 關愛長者：守護長者權益
 工作坊 (English &Chinese)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261106T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261106T160000
UID:6a905cc7be148200298a7306@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Break down the stigma around elder abuse and learn how to reco
 gnize and respond to it in a safe way. Join a facilitator from Theatre of 
 the Beat for this practical and engaging workshop where you'll learn to:\n
 identify the different types of elder abuse\nIdentify the signs of elder a
 buse\nUsing theatre exercises\, develop strategies to be an 'active bystan
 der' in situations where elder abuse might be present. No theatre experien
 ce required\, but participation in role plays is encouraged!\nAbout Theatr
 e of the Beat:\nTheatre of the Beat (TOTB) is a Canadian touring theatre c
 ompany working to catalyze conversations on social justice and its interse
 ction with the beliefs of the communities in which we find ourselves.\nLig
 ht refreshments are provided for all participants. Ingredient lists will b
 e available at the program.\n關愛長者：守護長者權益工作坊\n
 長者是家庭和社區的重要成員。我們都希望長者能夠生
 活得安心、受到尊重，並獲得適切的關懷與支持。\n歡迎
 參加這個互動工作坊，透過討論和戲劇活動，一起認識
 長者在日常生活中可能遇到的困難與挑戰，學習如何關
 心身邊的長者，並在適當的情況下提供支援和協助。\n您
 將有機會學習：\n了解影響長者身心健康及生活福祉的不
 同情況\n認識需要關注的徵兆和訊號\n透過戲劇練習及角
 色扮演，學習如何成為積極的旁觀者，以安全和尊重的
 方式關心及支持有需要的長者\n無需戲劇經驗，歡迎所有
 關心長者福祉的人士參加。活動鼓勵參加者輕鬆投入互
 動練習，在交流中學習實用的關愛技巧。\n關於 Theatre of 
 the Beat\nTheatre of the Beat（TOTB）是一家加拿大巡迴劇團，
 透過戲劇促進社區對社會議題的交流與對話，鼓勵大眾
 共同建立更關愛和包容的社區。\n活動將提供輕便茶點.
LOCATION:Steeles
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a905cc7be148200298a7306
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
